DTRACE COVERAGE: THIRTEENTH EXPANSION
====================================

20 operation-focused passes, 39 new probe contracts in 12 kernel source files,
and eight base-install bsdinstruments profiles. This is an additional gap
expansion, not a claim of complete coverage of every kernel subsystem or every
error path. No new USDT provider is added to a base executable in this round.

Prioritization and history
--------------------------
Existing syscall, VM completion, fd lifecycle, firmware lookup/load and VFS
mutation probes remain intact. New probes add missing semantic phases,
results and queue snapshots rather than replace those contracts.
Reviewed sys_procdesc.c history, including 891e1a37812 (remote descriptor
transfer policy), 425f274a444 (procdesc/proctree lock-order correction),
8b202b7ffe2 (unreachable NOTE_REAP removal), and the FreeBSD 16 merge
0632606e7fb. The new remote-transfer pair includes the actual fdup_remote
policy decision and reports its result; it adds no policy or locking change.

The 20 passes and their contracts
--------------------------------
The notation provider:::stem-{start,done} describes a synchronous pair.
Argument lists below are ordered. Identities are opaque, borrowed values;
consumers must not dereference them or assume they remain alive afterwards.
Completion errors are errno unless explicitly described as KERN_* results.

 1. procresource:::nice-{start,done}
    start(pid, requested_nice); done(pid, nice, error).
    Encloses donice's scheduling-permission check and sched_nice. The process
    lock is held. All three returns emit completion. nice on completion is
    clamped except when p_cansched rejects before clamping. This is one target
    operation, not the entire PRIO_USER/PRIO_PGRP syscall. Process lookup and
    visibility failures before donice are excluded.
 2. procresource:::limit-{start,done}
    start(resource, requested_soft, requested_hard); done(resource, error).
    Encloses kern_setrlimit's call to kern_proc_setrlimit, including validation,
    permissions, allocation and commit. Direct callers of kern_proc_setrlimit
    do not fire this pair. User copyin before kern_setrlimit is excluded.
 3. vfs:::extattr-get-{start,done}
    start(vnode, namespace); done(vnode, namespace, error).
    Begins after length validation, before vnode locking. Includes MAC check,
    VOP and unlock. No attribute name, value or output buffer is exposed.
 4. vfs:::extattr-list-{start,done}
    Same arguments. Begins after size setup/validation, before vnode locking;
    both MAC denial and ordinary return complete. No attribute names exposed.
 5. vfs:::acl-get-{start,done}
    start(vnode, acl_type); done(vnode, acl_type, error).
    Includes ACL allocation, vnode locking, MAC check, VOP, user copyout and
    freeing the temporary ACL. Path/fd resolution occurs before this phase.
 6. vfs:::acl-check-{start,done}
    Same arguments. Includes temporary allocation, copyin, VOP_ACLCHECK and
    cleanup. Tests ACL applicability; this is not a granted-access decision.
 7. procdescriptor:::open-{start,done}
    start(target_pid, flags); done(target_pid, flags, error).
    Encloses kern_pdopenpid, including falloc failure, visibility/security,
    procdesc setup, finstall and cleanup. Bad syscall flags rejected by the
    outer sys_pdopenpid wrapper are excluded. No new lifetime references.
 8. procdescriptor:::transfer-{start,done}
    start(procdesc_fd, source_fd); done(procdesc_fd, source_fd, error).
    Encloses kern_pddupfd: descriptor rights, process exit/debug checks,
    exec blocking, remote transfer policy, duplication and cleanup. The
    target process is not dereferenced after its reference is dropped.
    Invalid outer syscall flags are excluded; other transfer APIs are not
    automatically covered by this pair.
 9. sendfile:::io-wait-{start,done}
    start(sf_io_identity); done(sf_io_identity).
    Encloses the complete existing sendfile_iowait loop, including a no-wait
    invocation. It reports a wait phase, not disk I/O success. No probe in
    the low-level completion callback; no dereference of sf_io at the site.
10. sendfile:::socket-wait-{start,done}
    start(socket, bytes_needed); done(socket, error).
    One pair per sbwait invocation. Completion follows send-buffer lock
    reacquisition; a wake may be followed by another wait after rechecking.
    Nonblocking EAGAIN before sleeping is excluded.
11. fd:::advlock-{start,done}
    start(vnode, operation, flags); done(vnode, operation, error).
    Encloses VOP_ADVLOCK in file_advlock, including a blocking lock request's
    wait. Not every fcntl validation or every filesystem-internal lock call.
12. vm:::sync-map-{start,done}
    start(rounded_size, msync_flags); done(KERN_result).
    Encloses vm_map_sync after range/flag validation. Completion precedes
    syscall errno conversion. Includes synchronous/asynchronous mode choice;
    it is not an assertion that all data reached persistent media.
13. vm:::wire-map-{start,done}
    start(range_size); done(KERN_result).
    Encloses kern_mlock's vm_map_wire after privilege, range and accounting
    checks. Excludes those earlier failures, mlockall and later accounting
    rollback. Complements existing vm:::mlock completion.
14. socketctl:::protocol-{start,done}
    start(socket, ioctl_command); done(socket, ioctl_command, error).
    Encloses protocol pr_control dispatch in soo_ioctl with CURVNET set.
    Interface/routing branches and generic socket commands are excluded.
    No ioctl data, addresses or packet payloads are captured.
15. geomdev:::flush-{start,done}
    start(consumer); done(consumer, error).
    Encloses g_io_flush for DIOCGFLUSH in g_dev_ioctl. Reports that helper's
    completion, not every flush submitted elsewhere through GEOM.
16. geomdev:::delete-{start,done}
    start(consumer, chunk_offset, chunk_length); done(consumer, error).
    One pair per g_delete_data chunk after ioctl validation and alignment.
    Size-limited chunks are distinct operations. Invalid requests rejected
    before the chunk loop do not fire. No contents or keys are exposed.
17. geomdev:::close-wait-{start,done}
    start(softc, active_count); done(softc, active_count).
    One pair per existing msleep while the last close waits for active I/O.
    Counts exclude SC_A_OPEN/SC_A_DESTROY flag bits. A timeout/wake is not
    successful close: the original loop rechecks its condition. No invented
    errno, completion, retry or recovery claim.
18. firmware:::load-wait-{start,done}
    start(load_request_identity); done(load_request_identity).
    One pair around the existing fwload sleep inside !cold, after the task
    has been queued. Includes mutex reacquisition. A wake is not successful
    firmware registration; existing lookup/module/binary outcomes remain
    the authority for that. No firmware name, blob or early-boot probe added.
19. epair:::enqueue(queue_id, packet_length, resulting_depth, error)
    Runs under the existing queue mutex after mbufq_enqueue. Measures success
    or queue exhaustion, bytes attempted and resulting depth. Does not read
    mbuf after transfer; length was already saved by the original code.
    Queue IDs are local to epair instances: the profile deliberately
    aggregates matching queue IDs across pairs/VNETs, not per-interface
    identity. Receiving/processing completion is not covered here.
20. vfs:::copy-range-{start,done}
    start(input_vnode, output_vnode, requested_length);
    done(input_vnode, output_vnode, returned_length, error).
    Encloses vn_copy_file_range with both range locks already acquired.
    Excludes earlier fd/range checks and range-lock waiting, plus later
    descriptor offset handling. returned_length is the helper's value; use
    the error argument when interpreting it, particularly on partial errors.

Nine-category mapping
---------------------
Lifecycle/execution: operation entry/completion across the 19 paired phases.
Performance/latency: wall time between paired boundaries, including preemption.
Errors/exceptions: helper errno, permission denials, KERN_* results, queue errors.
Resources: limit requests/results, memory wiring, procdesc acquisition/transfer,
           epair queue exhaustion and depth.
I/O: GEOM flush/delete, VM sync, file copying and sendfile I/O waiting.
Networking: sendfile socket capacity, protocol control and epair enqueue.
Synchronization: sendfile, GEOM close and firmware waits; VOP advisory locks.
Security/access: scheduling/limit permission results, procdesc rights/debug/
                 transfer outcomes, MAC denial on metadata reads.
Counters/state: epair queue depth and attempted length, GEOM active counts,
                nice outcomes and resource-limit requests.

No synthetic deadlock, health, retry, timeout, authentication or recovery
probe is added where the underlying code does not establish that event.
The nine categories apply collectively; each subsystem has only relevant
categories. Some access errors require correlation with existing MAC/capsicum
probes to distinguish a specific denial from other errno causes.

Safety and overhead
-------------------
New sites are in ordinary operation or sleepable driver control paths.
No raw interrupt entry, NMI, allocator core, panic, scheduler context-switch,
SMR fast lookup or early-boot initialization site was added. Sites add no
allocation, lock acquisition, clock read or payload copy. Disabled SDT hooks
eliminate arguments, including the epair queue-depth accessor. With hooks
compiled in but disabled, the native amd64 sites are inactive NOPs. Enabled
packet and metadata probes may be high volume; enable selected profiles.
Existing synchronization protects the values sampled here. Probe identities
are borrowed and may be reused. No credential contents are newly collected.

Base profiles
-------------
  kernel-resource-decisions    nice and limit outcomes/latency
  kernel-procdesc-operations   open and remote-transfer outcomes/latency
  vfs-metadata-reads          ACL and extended-attribute phases
  kernel-data-waits           sendfile and advisory-lock phases
  vm-operation-phases        map sync/wiring with raw KERN_* results
  geom-device-operations      flush, delete chunks and close waits
  kernel-device-control      protocol control, firmware wait, epair queue
  vfs-copy-phases             copy helper outcomes/latency

The existing bsdinstruments Makefile discovers these .d files for the base
package at /usr/share/bsdinstruments/profiles; the install list has 396 entries.
Nothing was installed. Example against a kernel with the providers present:
  bsdinstruments generate -f /usr/share/bsdinstruments/profiles/kernel-data-waits.d

Profiles use pid/tid/depth pairing, aggregate numeric outcomes and latency,
and clear matching state. PID filters select executing context, not target
procdesc PID or network peer. Begin tracing before a workload; enabling
mid-operation, dropped events, thread exits and unmatched starts can make
pairing incomplete. Latency is elapsed time, not CPU service time. Offline
-Z compilation is accompanied by exact selector/argument and ELF-site checks.

Validation and reproducibility
------------------------------
Prepared native amd64 GENERIC-NODEBUG under /usr/obj; all 16 CPUs used for
parallel compilation. Scripts are under tools/test/system-tracing:
  coverage-thirteenth-compile.py: 12 sources x hooks on/off, -Werror.
  coverage-thirteenth-variants.py: 6 sources x hooks on/off, with MAC,
    RACCT, COMPAT_FREEBSD32 and RSS undefined for alternate branches.
    Existing kern_mprotect unused local 'error' breaks -Werror when MAC is
    off on both saved baseline and edited VM source. Only that variant
    source demotes unused-variable to a warning. Normal builds have no waiver.
    Initial failures and baseline reproduction are retained in variant-initial.
  coverage-thirteenth-modules.py: firmware.ko and if_epair.ko linked.
  Prepared kernel linked with make -j16 kernel (not installed).
  coverage-thirteenth-check.py: all 39 contract sites, executable NOPs,
    no-hooks absence, multi-exit sites, source hashes, install membership and
    8 profiles x normal/PID-duration filtered offline DTrace compilation.
  coverage-thirteenth-codegen.py: 12 baseline/current no-hooks .text byte
    comparisons, with diagnostic __FILE__/__LINE__ normalized. All match.
    This does not establish relocation equivalence or runtime behavior.

Artifacts and per-file before images:
  /usr/obj/5bsd-dtrace-thirteenth-validation
Session-only patch and SHA256 are retained there and reverse-checked against
this source tree. No unrelated dirty changes are part of the session patch.
No /dev/dtrace is available. Live firing, event ordering under contention,
traffic/storage workloads and overhead remain unqualified. No module load,
interface creation, firmware request, disk ioctl, publication, install,
upgrade or reboot was performed. This pass does not erase earlier runtime
qualification gaps or replace the broader coverage census.
